   "prompt": "<p>For the following exercises, consider the gamma function given by $\\Gamma (a)=\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-y}y^{a-1}dy .$</p><p>Extend to show that $\\Gamma (a)=(a-1)!,$ assuming $a$ is a positive integer.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "solution": "First, $\\Gamma (1)=\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-y}dy=\\lim_{t\\to \\infty}(1-e^{-t})=1=0!.$ For a positive integer $a,$ applying $\\Gamma (a)=(a-1)\\Gamma (a-1)$ again and again gives $\\Gamma (a)=(a-1)(a-2)\\cdots 1\\cdot \\Gamma (1)=(a-1)!.$",
    "generated_by": "ai"
